text:
Good context to establish before diving into the local implementation. Walking through the real code paths (I read the actual SDK source earlier this session, not guessing):

**1. Write path — tracking a view (PDP mount)**

```ts
const einstein = useEinstein();
useEffect(() => {
  einstein.sendViewProduct(product);
}, [product.id]);
```

This POSTs to `/v3/activities/{siteId}/viewProduct` with `{product: {id, sku, altId, altIdType}, cookieId, realm, instanceType}`. The `cookieId` is the same `usid` SFCC's SLAS auth already maintains via cookie — the wrapper hook injects it into every call automatically (`cookieId: [REDACTED] getUsidWhenReady()`). For a logged-in shopper it also sends `userId` (an encrypted customer ID). Neither of those is something our own code manages — they're just read from the existing auth state and passed through.

**2. Where the history actually lives**

Nowhere in our code. Einstein's own servers are the "database" — every `sendViewProduct` call appends to that `cookieId`'s (or `userId`'s, for logged-in continuity) view history, server-side. This is the fundamental architectural difference from what we built: Einstein's model has no client-side storage at all.

**3. Read path — displaying the rail**

```ts
const { recs } = await einstein.getRecommendations('viewed-recently-einstein', [], {});
// recs: [{ id, product_name, image_url, product_url }, ...]
const ids = recs.map(r => r.id);
const { data: products } = useProducts(ids); // real SFCC Commerce API call
```

This is exactly the pattern the base SFCC code already uses for the *other* Einstein recommenders (Might-Also-Like, Complete-the-Set) — `RecommendedProducts` already has this branch, untouched, for those. `getRecommendations` returns IDs plus some Einstein-cached metadata (name/image/url), but that metadata isn't used for display — it can be stale or point at the wrong domain (confirmed this session against the demo tenant). The IDs get re-hydrated through `useProducts`, the same real Commerce API call our current build already makes. **This step is identical either way** — the only thing that differs between an Einstein-backed and our browser-storage-backed design is *where the list of "which IDs were recently viewed" comes from*.

**4. Cap/order — not fully client-controlled either way**

We confirmed live this session that `getRecommendations` doesn't honor a client-requested limit — the BM-configured recommender decides how many it returns and in what order. So even with Einstein, the frontend would likely still need its own display-side capping (same `RECENTLY_VIEWED_RAIL_VISIBLE_COUNTS` logic we already built), just not the "which IDs count as recently viewed at all" bookkeeping — that part Einstein owns entirely.

**5. Cross-device continuity (AC 7)**

This is the one thing Einstein's model gets for free that browser storage structurally can't: `userId` ties the same view history to a logged-in shopper across devices, since the state lives server-side, not in one browser's `localStorage`.

That's the full real mechanism — happy to get into your concern on the local implementation now.
